1. The Compounding Velocity (Ταχύ / Tachy): Modern models describe a linear progression as constant and predictable. Revelation 22:7 uses the term Ταχύ (Tachy)—the root of our modern tachometer—to denote a rate of change that accelerates rapidly once the tripwires snap. It represents a velocity that continuously outruns the system’s chronological buffers.
2. The Frictional Multiplier (Διπλόω / Diploō): In Revelation 18:6, the text demands a compounded squaring of systemic feedbacks: “διπλώσατε τὰ διπλᾶ” (render to her double, doubled). This is the exact grammatical structure used to represent a curve that bends vertically. The consequences fold back into the system, doubling the intensity with every consecutive iteration.
3. The Fraction Inversion (From 1/4 to 1/3 to Totality): The ultimate linguistic plotting of an exponential slope is found in the rapid erasure of fractions across the book's three judgment series. Destruction begins capped at one-fourth (τὸ τέταρτον—Rev 6:8), immediately bends sharply upward to one-third (τὸ τρίτον—Rev 8:7-12), and then completely breaches the threshold into total, absolute system failure (Πᾶσα / Pasa—Rev 16:3). The fractions disappear as the system moves past linear stress into a runaway cascade.
